* soft-start -- the ideal-ramp A/B named by design/softstart_injection_compensation.md
* section 4 (issue #246, DR-0023's follow-on).
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* The question this deck exists to answer
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* design/softstart_injection_compensation.md section 4 leaves one thing open
* and names the experiment that would settle it:
*
*   At tt/-40 C/2.97 V the output the loop has to acquire at hold release
*   differs between the ideal element and the device chain by a factor of
*   2.1, but the capacitor current it acquires it with differs by 356x. A
*   2.1x difference in the voltage cannot by itself produce a 356x difference
*   in the current, so the transient is NOT the DC transfer defect scaled up.
*
*   "The next experiment, named so it does not have to be re-invented: a
*    transient A/B in sim/soft-start/ with V(SSR) driven by an *ideal* ramp
*    source through the same promoted SSR port this experiment adds, instead
*    of by Fss_ramp. That separates the injection element's transfer from the
*    ramp's own dynamics and from the hold release[.]"
*
* This deck is that A/B. It is the SAME circuit, the SAME stimulus, the SAME
* output network and the SAME load as tb_soft_start.spice.in -- the bench
* whose records adjudicate the ratified Startup row -- with exactly one thing
* changed between the two arms:
*
*   arm A (`internal`)  V(SSR) is generated the way the design generates it:
*                       Fss_ramp's current into Css, released by Mdis_ss when
*                       SD decays, ceilinged by Mtop_ss. The promoted SSR port
*                       is left UNCONNECTED, exactly as the loop-gain bench's
*                       `anchor` phase leaves it, so this arm is electrically
*                       tb_soft_start.spice.in.
*   arm B (`ideal`)     V(SSR) is FORCED by an ideal PWL voltage source on the
*                       promoted port. Fss_ramp/Css/Mdis_ss/Mtop_ss are still
*                       present and still connected -- an ideal source simply
*                       absorbs whatever they push -- so the two arms differ in
*                       the ramp node's DYNAMICS and in nothing else. The hold
*                       release (Rh_ss/Ch_ss -> HG), the FB pre-charge and the
*                       injection element are untouched in both arms.
*
* The PWL is generated by ramp_ab.py from arm A's OWN measured ramp, so the
* two arms traverse the same V(SSR) at the same times to within the fit: the
* A/B is "same trajectory, different source impedance and different
* higher-order dynamics", not "different ramp rate". A slope difference would
* confound the comparison with the ramp-rate axis DR-0006/DR-0024 already own.
*
* WHY THE SSR PORT IS PROMOTED BY A DERIVED NETLIST AND NOT IN design/
* The port promotion is a pure text transform of design/netlist/ldo_core.spice
* performed by sim/soft-start-loop-gain/testbench/netlist_variants.py
* (promote_ssr_port/instrument_core, issue #196). SSR is already a node of the
* cell, so naming it in a port list renames nothing, connects nothing and
* creates nothing. ramp_ab.py calls that same implementation rather than
* carrying a second copy, and freezes the derived netlist next to the record.
*
* Template: the at-sign-delimited placeholders are substituted by ramp_ab.py.

.include "@DESIGN_INCLUDE@"
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MOS_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @RES_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @BJT_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @DIODE_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MOSCAP_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MIMCAP_CORNER@

.temp @TEMP_C@

* Per-finger model binning -- see docs/environment-setup.md and DR-0025.
* Without it the pass device (W=2000u nf=40) resolves to no declared bin and
* ngspice hard-errors.
.options wnflag=1

.include "@LDO_NETLIST@"

* --- stimulus (identical to tb_soft_start.spice.in) ----------------------
Vin VIN 0 DC @VIN_V@
Ven EN 0 DC 0 PWL(0 0 100u 0 101u @VIN_V@ 8m @VIN_V@ 8.001m 0)
Vref VREF 0 DC 1.2

* --- DUT, with the promoted SSR port -------------------------------------
* Port order: VIN VOUT EN VSS ERRAMP_OUT PASS_GATE VREF SSR
Xdut VIN VOUT EN 0 EOUT PG VREF SSRPIN ldo_core
Vloop PG EOUT DC 0

* --- the one thing that differs between the two arms ---------------------
@SSR_DRIVE@

* --- output network (identical to tb_soft_start.spice.in) ----------------
Resr VOUT n_esr @ESR_OHM@
Cout n_esr n_cap @COUT@
Vcmeas n_cap 0 DC 0

* --- load (identical to tb_soft_start.spice.in) --------------------------
Rload VOUT LN @RLOAD_OHM@
Vlmeas LN 0 DC 0

.options reltol=1e-3 vntol=1e-5 abstol=1e-11 chgtol=1e-14

.control
set noaskquit

echo "* corner_id=@CORNER_ID@ arm=@ARM@ temp_c=@TEMP_C@ vin_v=@VIN_V@"
echo "* cout=@COUT@ esr=@ESR_OHM@ rload=@RLOAD_OHM@"

tran 1u 9m
let icap_ma = i(vcmeas)*1e3
let ivin_ma = -i(vin)*1e3
let dvout_vpms = deriv(v(vout))*1e-3

* --- the ramp node itself, so arm B's PWL can be fitted to arm A ---------
* Two fixed SSR crossings well inside the ramp: the pair is exactly what
* ramp_ab.py turns into arm B's slope and time origin. The node is read at
* the PROMOTED PORT (`v(ssrpin)`), not at `v(xdut.xsoftstart.ssr)` the way
* tb_soft_start.spice.in reads it: once SSR is a port of both ldo_core and
* ldo_softstart it maps onto the top-level net the port is wired to, and the
* hierarchical name no longer resolves. Same node either way -- a port is a
* rename, not a new net -- so the crossings are the same crossings that
* bench measures.
meas tran m_ssr_t02 WHEN v(ssrpin)=0.2 RISE=1
meas tran m_ssr_t10 WHEN v(ssrpin)=1.0 RISE=1
meas tran m_ssr_end FIND v(ssrpin) AT=7.9m

* --- the Startup-row clauses, same measures as tb_soft_start.spice.in ----
meas tran m_t_v04 WHEN v(vout)=0.4 RISE=1
meas tran m_t_v14 WHEN v(vout)=1.4 RISE=1
meas tran m_t_startup TRIG v(en) VAL=1.4 RISE=1 TARG v(vout) VAL=1.764 RISE=1
meas tran m_vout_max_en MAX v(vout) FROM=101u TO=8m
meas tran m_vout_settled FIND v(vout) AT=7.9m
meas tran m_icap_peak_ma MAX icap_ma FROM=100u TO=8m
meas tran m_isup_peak_ma MAX ivin_ma FROM=100u TO=8m
meas tran m_dvout_max MAX dvout_vpms FROM=105u TO=8m
meas tran m_dvout_min MIN dvout_vpms FROM=105u TO=8m
meas tran m_hg_end FIND v(xdut.xsoftstart.hg) AT=7.9m

* --- the hold-release acquisition window, which is what section 4 is about
* Mhold_ss releases PASS_GATE ~1.3 tau into the enable window (tau ~29.4 us
* post-DR-0024), so 100-600 us after the enable edge is the window the
* acquisition transient lives in. Reporting it separately from the
* whole-window peak is what makes "the spike is at hold release" a measured
* statement rather than an inference from a single global maximum.
meas tran m_icap_peak_acq_ma MAX icap_ma FROM=101u TO=700u
meas tran m_vout_at_hr FIND v(vout) AT=700u
meas tran m_ssr_at_hr FIND v(ssrpin) AT=700u
* ... and the rest of the ramp, so the two can be told apart.
meas tran m_icap_peak_ramp_ma MAX icap_ma FROM=700u TO=8m

* The marker text is `SWEEP COMPLETE` and not something arm-specific because
* sim/harness/runner.py's run_ngspice_deck() -- the shared "run one deck and
* validate it" wrapper this driver uses, so that a fatal ngspice condition
* reported while still exiting 0 cannot be mistaken for a clean run -- looks
* for exactly that string. The corner/arm suffix is this deck's own, and is
* what ramp_ab.py matches so an arm cannot be credited with its sibling's log.
echo "* SWEEP COMPLETE @CORNER_ID@ @ARM@"
.endc
.end
